  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1030 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nSunday through Friday:\n\nLow pressure tracking east across Hudson Bay Canada this weekend \nwill drag a cold front into WI. The front should reach\nnorthwestern portions of WI by late Saturday night into Sunday\nmorning, with strong high pressure over Saskatchewan then\nforcing the front across southern WI later Sunday through Sunday\nnight. Predominantly dry weather is expected to accompany the\nfrontal passage, and the late arrival of the front allows\ndaytime highs to reach the low 70s Sunday. CAA behind the front\nkicks in late Sunday night into Monday, with northeasterly \nwinds off the lake. As such, central and eastern WI are forecast\nto observe highs in the 50s, with highs in the mid 60s further\nwest along the southern edge of the state. \n\nMeanwhile on Monday, a 500mb shortwave trough will dig into the\ncentral Great Plains, phasing with the aforementioned frontal \nboundary to seed surface low pressure. This low pressure looks \nto lift northeast into Monday night, bringing some 5-15% rain \nchances to the CWA. The bulk of the QPF and higher precip \nchances with this system land in MN and northwestern WI, \nleaving us predominantly dry as it passes. The remnant \nbaroclinic zone from that activity then sets the stage for an \nadditional upper trough and surface low to slide northeast from \nthe Plains midweek. The placement of precip with this second \nsystem is less certain, but could track through the same \ncorridor of MN / NW-WI or further east. ~15-30% precip chances \nare forecast for the CWA Wednesday until ensembles converge on a\nsystem track. In any scenario, we will be in the warm sector of\nboth passing systems, resulting in a warming trend towards\nmidweek. \n\nThis same pattern looks to continue later this week, with\nadditional rain chances. \n\nSheppard",
